          "Reflect: Paul's case in Romans 4 is that Abraham was justified before his circumcision — before any act of religious observance, before any work that could claim merit. He \"believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness\" (v. 3). This is the structure of justification: God counts (credits, imputes) righteousness to the one who believes. What difference does it make that righteousness is *credited* to you rather than *grown* in you or *infused* by a ritual? How would your practice of prayer, confession, and assurance change if you were more convinced that justification is a declaration already complete rather than a process still in progress?*",
